41}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c3886db1-970f-5bef-b60d-504d892dccac" :question/math-academy-id "q-333328" :question/problem "![](images/33/330e2a1dd9f7d2cbcadb61fe49e767d95ee76a2cb669a9917ef3f6333719b98f.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$<0.$" :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c675f557abede7c8f21eadf3fd65dbd522c5ee9ad0a4ed0a804b03f357c1dc3b.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a2/a25c57a021e42b2fa78b71f9ab79f0676a16f7c4f1082af0e9502d4d63ed4e46.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are negative. The eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is the one closest to $0.$\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{2}$ as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior is ${\\lambda }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{1}<{\\lambda }_{2}<0.$"} :step/id #uuid "890e1bbb-beae-5fdb-be53-89bb4d05b48c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445875}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445874 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707684fd-d61c-5ad5-987f-4be798154167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306223} {:answer/id #uuid "c158e131-b3d3-5c44-8b42-3e8ab79bb634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17